entry, not a reset or continuation of a stopped Budget. Global defaults and
per-page parsing/lowering limits are unchanged.

## Checks and limits

An earlier five-page trial passed Markdown but HTML stopped at default Work100M,
before creating the HTML output directory. The raw command/log is retained as an
initial observation: it does not bind a firmware/executable hash or claim a
fixed-commit differential benchmark. With the separately selected configuration,
two six-file Markdown stages and two seven-file HTML bundles are byte-identical.
A subsequent guarded run at fixed clean `799f00d` passed canonical checking and
regenerated both formats; all six/seven files equal the earlier output bytes.

Root browser checks passed in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit with JavaScript disabled
at widths375/1280 under two non-root paths. Twelve displays retained the table,
Ruby/Anno and both inline-code values; 48 real semantic fragment moves passed.
Root inspected the narrow WebKit screenshot. Nine actual GitHub targets (five
legacy plus four semantic) were individually navigated with exact server rawLines,
unique targets and stable visible positions. HTML has semantic Section IDs, not
the Markdown-only legacy alias names.

The first GitHub script accidentally printed a copied message saying15; its JSON
contains nine actual tests. The original script/results and correction are kept.
The final script asserts and reports nine and passed again. No source, alias or
expected target was changed to fix this reporting mistake.

Independent manuscript review checked the entire original, table cells, code,
Ruby and semantic boundaries before generation. The original source date and
historical commit are preserved as context, not treated as the current HEAD.
Code/generation, browser/cutover and archive integration reviews have separate
records and scope. This is a page cutover, not completed HTML/KaTeX products,
T21/T16, live Pages deployment, human accessibility or physical-browser testing.

## Read checked HTML without building the compiler

From the repository root, run this exact snippet to restore a new directory.
It uses neither Rust nor Node nor network generation.

```python
from pathlib import Path
import hashlib, json

archive = Path("conformance/results/doc-canonical-html-delivery")
output = Path("dist/canonical18-snapshot")
entries = json.loads((archive / "payloads.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))["files"]
selected = [e for e in entries if e["owner"] == "root-html-first"]
assert len(selected) == 7
output.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=False)
for entry in selected:
    relative = Path(entry["restore"])
    assert not relative.is_absolute() and ".." not in relative.parts
    data = (archive / entry["file"]).read_bytes()
    assert len(data) == entry["bytes"]
    assert hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest() == entry["sha256"]
    destination = output / relative
    destination.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with destination.open("xb") as stream:
        stream.write(data)
```

Open `dist/canonical18-snapshot/docs/spec/18-html-delivery.html` with its adjacent
assets and pages. This reading snapshot does not replace regeneration checks.
The payload index preserves original paths, byte lengths and SHA-256. Reviewer
files come from strict manifests, excluding build workspaces/caches/binaries.
`.fixture` stores original bytes; reserved archive path components use `saved-*`.
